starFlagT -- `*x.flagName` IS NOT A FLAG READ, AND THIS IS THE ACCEPTANCE FIXTURE FOR
THE CHAINED-DOT CHANGE. Minted 2026-09-15 on Tony's ruling. Its rows are what FLIP when
`.` becomes a binary left-associative operator; until then they pin what the language
does today, which is not what anyone reading `*x.flag` expects.

WHY IT EXISTS. incant/fixits/faceFlagsNoCross spelt its forward row `*fcFace.noPrinT`
and read 1; incant/pop/faceT asks the identical question as `faFace.noPrinT` and reads
0. One character, opposite answers, and the citizen was one commit from retiring as
"fixed" on the strength of it.

⚠⚠ THE ROW THAT SETTLES IT IS SF-6, AND IT IS WHY THE CONTROLS ARE ARRANGED THIS WAY.
SF-1 against SF-2 is only a disagreement -- it says one of them is wrong and not which.
SF-5 and SF-6 ask the SAME question of the SOURCE FIELD THE FLAG WAS JUST WRITTEN TO,
where the answer is not in doubt. The plain read says 1. The starred read returns the
local's own TAG -- no data at all. A spelling that cannot read a flag off the field that
carries it is not reading flags anywhere, so SF-2's 1 is noise rather than an answer.

    read                 NO star        WITH star
    the face             0              1
    never written        0              tag echo
    the SOURCE           1              tag echo

⚠ THE TAG ECHOES ARE PINNED BY VALUE, DELIBERATELY, and this is not a bear-trap #26
re-offence. Pinning an echo as if it were data is the trap; pinning it as the EVIDENCE
THAT THERE IS NO DATA is the assertion, and it is presence-with-value rather than
absence-of-message (rule H4) -- the row prints a value on every run and compares it.
The moment the starred read starts returning a number, these two rows go red and
somebody re-reads. That is the whole job.

⚠⚠ SF-0 IS LOAD-BEARING, AND UNTIL 2026-09-16 IT COMPARED THE WRONG PAIR. It read the
ORIGINAL against the HOLDER and concluded from their different bodies that a flag must
not cross. THE HOLDER IS NOT THE FACE. The face is what the holder POINTS AT -- `*sfFace`
-- and it is now printed as SF-0's third row, where it reads body #2: THE SAME BODY AS THE
ORIGINAL, because `+%` attached a copy over it. Rule H13's question 1 -- is this the same
thing reached through a carrier -- reads the BODY column, and read correctly it says #2 on
both sides.

    1 the ORIGINAL          field #1   body #2
    2 the HOLDER <- minted  field #3   body #4    <- the old comparison stopped here
    3 THE FACE  *sfFace     field #5   body #2    <- and this is the one that matters

⚠ SO SF-2's 1 IS THE FLAG CROSSING LAWFULLY between two fields over one body, and it was
never noise. That wrong pair is how this file's prediction got made, and F-71's headline
was withdrawn on it.

MECHANISM, and it is already recorded rather than new: bear-trap #48's second half. `.`
is a member of the UnaryOPS bin as well as being registered opDot, so `*a.b` is TWO terms
and KANT-43's right-to-left association applies the star LAST -- `*(a.b)` -- a star
applied to a flag's VALUE. Banked as bear-trap #26 payment seven, which cross-references
this file.

THE PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION IS RESOLVED, 2026-09-16, AND IT FAILED -- WITH THE CONTROL
INTACT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A RESULT AND NOT A SHRUG. It said that once `.` folded,

    SF-2  1        -> 0     the face, dereferenced, still does not share the body
    SF-4  tag echo -> 0     the control reads an unset flag honestly
    SF-6  tag echo -> 1     THE ROW TO WATCH: the source reads its own set flag

The fold LANDED -- incant/pop/dotChainT, DC-2 and DC-4 went from `xl1` to LEAFVAL -- and
NONE OF THE THREE MOVED. ⚠ SF-1, SF-3 and SF-5 did not move either, and that is the clause
that decides how to read it: the treatment never reached this fixture's read machinery, so
this is a GENUINE NEGATIVE RESULT and not a voided control.

⚠ WHY IT COULD NOT HAVE MOVED, measured rather than argued. Every row here is a SINGLE
dot-COMPOSED term carrying its star as the term's own unary, so handleDot's
starDotRotation -- built 2026-09-07 -- already applies and interpretXP's juxtaposition is
never reached. The chain fold cannot touch these rows and NEITHER COULD ANY OTHER FOLD.
The prediction's premise was SF-0's wrong pair, corrected above.

WHAT THE ROWS MEAN NOW, and both halves are rulings rather than defects:

    SF-2   1        the flag crossing LAWFULLY -- original and face share body #2
    SF-4   no data  NULL BY RULING
    SF-6   no data  NULL BY RULING

⚠⚠ THE STAR RULING (Tony, 2026-09-05, re-affirmed 2026-09-16) lives at
Instruct.opDeref.starRuling and is what SF-4 and SF-6 report: `*x` on a field that holds no
group YIELDS NULL AND DOES NOT REFUSE -- refusal is for category errors, and asking a field
what it holds is a legitimate question with a legitimate empty answer, so `if *x` must be
able to take its else arm without ending the activation. THE CONSUMER OF THE NULL REFUSES,
and aCTionIterate's `nullAfterStar` arm is where that name is spoken. The ruling records
what the other way round cost: f31's spin was diagnosed for a day as a star problem because
the star was the thing shouting.

⚠ A PROVISIONAL RULING TO REFUSE HERE WAS RAISED AND WITHDRAWN ON 2026-09-16, and the
number behind the withdrawal is worth keeping: 36 live `*argument` sites read isGrouP 0 and
work today BY THIS DESIGN. Overturning it is a design change with those sites behind it,
not a fixture repair. `isGrouP` -- the GroupFields accessor minted in the same stroke --
predicts the star's answer exactly (1 iff the star yields something) and is the instrument
that made all of this legible.

⚠⚠ AND THE THIRD OUTCOME, NAMED BECAUSE THIS FIXTURE IS EXACTLY THE CASE THAT NEEDS IT:
THE TREATMENT CAN VOID THE CONTROL. The chained-dot change touches this fixture's own
read machinery, so a run where SF-5 or SF-1 moves does not mean the prediction failed --
it means the discriminators stopped discriminating and every row below them is
UNINTERPRETABLE rather than wrong. Report that as a voided control. Do not grade it.
(Ratified 2026-08-10 on the KANT-8 bracket rung, which failed in precisely this way.)

